The two parental inbred haplotypes are \(00000\) and \(11111\). Exactly one crossover occurs per meiosis, so the four possible intervals between the five SNPs produce exactly eight single-transition haplotypes in the F2 generation:

\[
00001,\;00011,\;00111,\;01111,\;10000,\;11000,\;11100,\;11110.
\]

Every F2 individual therefore carries a pair drawn from this set. The single obligatory crossover that generates each F3 haplotype assembles a prefix from one member of the pair and the complementary suffix from the other. Because each input chromosome already contains at most one transition, the resulting F3 string can contain at most two transitions in total. Exhaustive enumeration of all admissible pairs and crossover positions shows that every binary string of length 5 possessing 0, 1 or 2 transitions is realized at least once:

- 0 transitions: the two parental strings (restored by compensating crossovers);
- 1 transition: the original eight strings;
- 2 transitions: \(\binom{4}{2}\times2=12\) strings.

No string with three or more transitions can appear after only two successive single-crossover events, and the single-orientation/positive-strand restriction does not remove any of the 22 reachable patterns. Hence exactly 22 distinct sequences exist in the F3 generation.

