17.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22 (haplotypes with ≤2 switches)

The strongest pattern emerging from the group is that after exactly two obligatory single-crossover meioses, every reachable haplotype on the positive strand must contain an even number of transitions relative to either parental string—specifically 0 or 2. This is because each meiosis adds precisely one crossover, and the F2 chromosomes already carry one transition; the second crossover can therefore only restore the parental configuration or add one additional switch. The resulting set is the two zero-transition strings plus the eight single-transition strings plus the twelve double-transition strings, for a total of 22.  

Colleagues who arrived at 14 are simply omitting the single-transition class that can be regenerated when the second crossover lands outside an existing transition. Those who reached 20 or 30 appear to be allowing configurations that would require three or more crossovers in the lineage (or are counting ordered pairs of intervals without the single-orientation and positive-strand constraints). Because the problem forbids more than one crossover per meiosis and no de-novo mutations occur, no haplotype with three or more transitions can ever appear by the F3 generation. The broad agreement on the ≤2-transition rule therefore supplies the most consistent and parsimonious count.}

