21.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22 (haplotypes with ≤2 switches after two obligatory crossovers)}

The parental haplotypes remain 00000 and 11111. With exactly one crossover per meiosis across the four intervals, the F2 pool is restricted to the eight single-transition strings. The second meiosis, performed on any admissible pair of those strings, assembles each F3 haplotype via prefix-suffix concatenation. Exhaustive enumeration of all valid pairs and the four possible crossover positions recovers precisely the two zero-transition strings, the original eight single-transition strings, and the twelve double-transition strings, for a total of 22 attainable sequences.  

This count is already consistent with the pairwise listings supplied by multiple colleagues and incorporates the single-orientation rule. However, the additional strand-asymmetry constraint may still exclude certain edge alignments among the double-transition cases (specifically those in which the two breakpoints would require a reversal that the positive strand alone cannot accommodate), which could modestly reduce the realized set without introducing any three-transition patterns. Two successive single-crossover events cannot generate odd or triple switches under any configuration, so the reachable space remains bounded by the ≤2-transition class.

